     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 5,100 mg/day of metformin is twice the maximum labeled dose, and I can't think of a clinical situation where it would be appropriate. The main risk is serious harm from metformin-associated lactic acidosis, and it would also likely cause severe GI effects. Normal renal and hepatic function doesn't make up for that.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **If it was a typo or a miscalculation,** I can enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum). I can also enter a lower dose if you'd rather titrate up.\n- **If you have a reason for this dose,** talk to your attending and the pharmacist first. If they agree, the order can go through a pathway with proper override and verification, not through me.\n- **If the goal is tighter glycemic control,** an inpatient insulin regimen or another added agent is safer than going above the maximum metformin dose. I can enter orders for those if you tell me what you want.\n\nTell me which option you want and I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
